---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const b = appBySlug('activecampaign')!;
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-activecampaign';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-activecampaign');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-activecampaign');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does ActiveCampaign fit this comparison?",
    "answer": "When cart email and a sales-owned quote or follow-up task belong in the same operating process. CRM automation actions require the applicable add-on; verify the purchased scope."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Postscript the proven recovery winner?",
    "answer": "No comparative merchant test is reported. Evaluate subscriber SMS recovery and its cancellation behavior against your own workflow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does an abandoned cart create a sales opportunity automatically?",
    "answer": "Not in the proposed design. Qualify quote requests and define ownership, deduplication and a marketing pause before creating a deal."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much does the proposed $725 incentive cost?",
    "answer": "At 42% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $420.50. A 14% code reduces gross profit from $304.50 to $203.00 before other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Postscript vs ActiveCampaign: reminder or sales-owned quote?</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">A stalled $725 basket may need a payment answer or a wholesale quote rather than a louder reminder. Choose who owns that next step before choosing the sending channel.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "SMS recovery", "tool": "Postscript", "reason": "Evaluate subscriber SMS flows when the shopper can complete the order without a sales quote."}, {"label": "Sales handoff", "tool": "ActiveCampaign", "reason": "Evaluate cart email plus a separately configured deal owner and follow-up task."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Separate an answerable question from a quote</h2>
<p>For the proposed $725 basket, a financing widget opened is only a clue. Ask whether the shopper needs payment information, a corrected bundle price or a negotiated wholesale order. Do not imply financing approval or promise terms that a sales owner has not approved. A below-MOQ basket should reach a named person who can explain quantities and quote validity. Keep the $163 self-service basket on a simpler recovery path unless the shopper actually requests help.</p>
<h2>ActiveCampaign: connect checkout evidence to an accountable owner</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360001045964-Abandoned-cart-overview">ActiveCampaign documents</a> Shopify cart email through Deep Data, a checkout-stage sync requirement, a configurable abandonment wait and a cart content block. A browse event alone does not establish eligibility. Inspect the integration clock before adding another delay and inspect the populated return link.</p>
<p>Its <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/218251828-Automation-actions-explained">automation action documentation</a> places CRM actions under the Pipelines and Sales Engagement add-ons. The <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360000028840-How-to-create-a-deal-in-ActiveCampaign">deal creation guide</a> exposes pipeline, stage and owner. Our proposed design qualifies an explicit quote request, creates or reconciles one opportunity and assigns a due task. Store the basket reference and agreed quote expiry. Pause promotional recovery while that owner is negotiating; resume only after a recorded decision. This is an implementation proposal, not an out-of-box Shopify quote sync claim.</p>
<h2>Postscript: keep self-service SMS tied to the right event</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564025-understanding-infinity-testing-in-automation-flows">Postscript’s flow guide</a> distinguishes product-added-to-cart from checkout-started triggers and their destination tags. It describes cancelling a checkout flow on order creation. That gives the SMS owner a concrete event and exit to inspect; it does not demonstrate a sales pipeline handoff. Test an eligible subscriber who completes checkout while a message waits, then verify cancellation and the actual destination.</p>
<h2>Rehearse the two buying paths</h2>
<p>Use a mobile newcomer and a repeat buyer. For mobile abandonment, inspect the return destination and changed quantities before adding urgency. For a repeat buyer already discussing terms, have the sales owner suppress the ordinary incentive. For an international shipping objection, obtain an accurate shipping quote rather than issuing a code that may not cover duties. For a subscription toggle, explain billing cadence before asking for completion.</p>
<p>Make one size unavailable, leave a gift message unfinished and apply a welcome offer to a bundle. Route sizing and gift questions to assistance, stop a fully unavailable basket, and check offer stacking before sending. Confirm how a sales reply reaches the owner and how an unresolved task prevents a second automated nudge. Reject the handoff if the task has no owner or if the same basket creates duplicate opportunities.</p>
<h2>Keep the original basket economics explicit</h2>
<p>These are proposed fixtures, not observed outcomes. The $725 basket at 42% gross margin has $420.50 fixed goods cost and $304.50 full-price gross profit. A 14% code removes $101.50 from revenue, leaving $623.50 revenue and $203.00 gross profit. The $163 basket at 40% margin has $97.80 goods cost and $65.20 gross profit. A 14% code removes $22.82, leaving $140.18 revenue and $42.38 gross profit. Neither percentage is a recommended cap. Exclude tax from the basket basis and account separately for shipping subsidies, payment fees, returns and staff time.</p>
<h2>Stage the replacement without competing reminders</h2>
<p>These are proposed acceptance checks, not an executed merchant trial. Start with a fresh, identifiable test shopper and channel-specific permission. Record the source event, store, basket, currency, destination URL and each queued message. Advance from browse to cart to checkout, then purchase during the wait. Verify that earlier queues stop and that no further recovery message remains. Repeat after an unsubscribe, stock change, refund and repeat purchase. A refund needs a service decision rather than an automatic restart of recovery.</p>
<p>Choose delays from your buying cycle and staffing coverage; no universal 36-minute or seven-hour ladder is established. Recheck stock, purchase state, permissions, quiet hours and other campaigns before dispatch. Give one operator responsibility for the combined email and SMS calendar. Pause the replaced recovery flow before enabling its successor, preserve configuration for rollback, and inspect the pending queue before resuming either system.</p>
<h2>Measure the decision rather than attributed revenue</h2>
<p>Randomize eligible shoppers into a treatment and a holdout sized for useful precision. Keep sales-assisted orders identifiable and use the same observation window. Compare incremental gross profit after discounts, refunds, message fees and staff cost, then account for platform and implementation costs. A proposed 5% holdout is a starting hypothesis, not a guarantee of adequate power. Platform-attributed purchases alone do not show how many shoppers would have returned anyway.</p>
<p>Product documentation checked November 25, 2026. Request current pricing for the audience, channels, usage, add-ons and implementation scope you actually need; no entry-price or rating ranking is asserted here.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
